/*
Problem: Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree (LC 236)
Topic: Binary Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

The Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) is the
lowest node that has both p and q in
its subtree (a node can be its own
ancestor).

Recursively search the left and right
subtrees.

Based on where p and q are found,
decide the LCA.

--------------------------------------------------

Cases:

1. Current node is NULL
   -> Return NULL.

2. Current node is p or q
   -> Return current node.

3. Search left and right subtree.

   • Both return non-NULL
     -> Current node is the LCA.

   • Only left is non-NULL
     -> LCA lies in left subtree.

   • Only right is non-NULL
     -> LCA lies in right subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

        3
      /   \
     5     1
    / \   / \
   6   2 0   8

p = 5
q = 1

Left recursion returns 5.
Right recursion returns 1.

Both are non-NULL.

Answer = 3

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The recursion searches both subtrees.

If p and q are found in different
subtrees, the current node is their
first meeting point, i.e., the LCA.

If both are in the same subtree,
that subtree's answer is propagated
upwards.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Every node is visited once.

Space : O(h)

h = height of tree
(O(log n) for balanced,
 O(n) for skewed tree)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Tree DFS + Postorder Recursion
*/

class Solution {
public:

    TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root,
                                   TreeNode* p,
                                   TreeNode* q) {

        // Base cases
        if (root == NULL)
            return NULL;

        if (root == p || root == q)
            return root;

        // Search both subtrees
        TreeNode* leftLCA = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);
        TreeNode* rightLCA = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);

        // p and q found in different subtrees
        if (leftLCA && rightLCA)
            return root;

        // LCA lies in left subtree
        if (leftLCA)
            return leftLCA;

        // LCA lies in right subtree
        return rightLCA;
    }
};
